+# FAA Crisis Center equipment assessment and Position 14 records
+
+**November 9, 2026 | B129 / PD03–PD04 / PD07.** Two open National Archives memoranda from December 17, 2003 identify a specific post-attack assessment of communications equipment and help separate the Eastern Region Crisis Command Center lines from the FAA headquarters and Herndon lines. The assessment itself and an equipment or channel assignment record remain unacquired. [NARA interview index](https://www.archives.gov/research/9-11/commission-memoranda.html#:~:text=2609832)
+
+## What the two interview records say
+
+- **Equipment assessment and operating records.** The McCartney memorandum reports that Crisis Center operators conducted a post-attack continuity-of-operations assessment covering communications and equipment performance. It separately reports McCartney's belief that lessons learned likely led to investment in the Domestic Events Network (DEN), with decisions probably made at headquarters. Preserve the attributed belief and scope. The memorandum identifies the operations-center manager as someone who might identify which lines were recorded. It supplies no equipment list, commissioning record, or manager's own statement. [NAID 2609833, physical p. 4, lines 20–38](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00241.pdf#page=4)
+- **Regional lines and action record.** The Ducharme memorandum says the Eastern Region Crisis Command Center lines were **unrecorded on September 11**. It separately describes asking for the center's actions to be recorded and attributes compilation of the FAA's **“Chronology of Events”** to the Region. The memo describes an action record and a line-recording account separately. Its underlying action notes, exact chronology version, and line coverage records remain open. The face gives `9/11` in the line-recording statement, while the text layer reads `9111`. [NAID 2609832, physical p. 2 lines 4–11, p. 4 lines 24–28](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00240.pdf#page=4)
+- **Before and after the Pentagon strike.** Ducharme's account puts a field telecom, tower checks and a report about Washington Center airspace shutdown in a passage ending **“well before the Pentagon was struck.”** McCartney describes a separate-room regional conference telecom and says it was established **“after the Pentagon was struck.”** The two descriptions identify candidate records with different wording and anchors. The speakers do not supply a shared circuit or activation record in those passages. Their relationship remains unresolved. [Ducharme memo, p. 2 lines 6–11](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00240.pdf#page=2) · [McCartney memo, p. 4 lines 7–14](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00241.pdf#page=4)
+- **Interview audio leads.** The McCartney MFR directs readers to an interview recording for further detail. The Ducharme MFR says most of his interview was unrecorded at his request. These notes refer to the 2003 interviews themselves. No interview recording was acquired in this review. [McCartney p. 1 line 18](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00241.pdf#page=1) · [Ducharme p. 1 line 20](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00240.pdf#page=1)
+
+## Position 14 names two candidate lines
+
+McCartney was unsure whether the telecom he described was called the FAA Tactical Net. Staff showed him a **Position 14 Transcript**. On the basis of the FBI appearing in it, the memorandum attributes to him a qualified view that it likely represented a different telecom from the headquarters telecom set up in the Eastern Region Crisis Center. It also reports a possibility that the Tactical Net was dialed into from the center. These are views recorded in the interview. The exact transcript copy presented, the line assignment, and the physical connection require verification. [McCartney p. 3 lines 29–40 and p. 4 lines 3–6](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00241.pdf#page=3)
+
+| Official FAA finding-aid parent | Printed position and window | Digitized child IDs in the index |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **DCC-1911**, Box 27 | **5154 / Position 14 East Area / 1215–1630 UTC** | **7598739**, **7598740**, **7598741** |
+| **DCC-1938**, Box 28 | **5114 / Position 14 East Area / 1215–1630 UTC** | **7598795**, **7598796**, **7598797** |
+
+The three child title ranges in each series are **1215–1415**, **1415–1615**, and **1615–1630 UTC**. The labels share a position and interval but identify different line designations. The public finding aid also lists a Team 8, Box 1 **ATCSCC Tape Transcript: Position 14** folder. The held archive member index lists a Team 8 four-part transcript and a separate NYC Box 1 transcript whose filename specifies **14B** and **5154**. The title and position alone leave the interview's copy unassigned. No transcript body, audio or clock comparison was performed in this unit. [FAA boxes 24–35, DCC-1911 and DCC-1938 rows](https://www.archives.gov/research/foia/faa-finding-aid-24-35) · [Commission finding aid, Team 8 Box 1, physical p. 111 line 39](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/finding-aid.pdf#page=111) · [Public archive index source](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip)
+
+## Keep the contrary accounts and custodians separate
+
+The June 2003 headquarters visit note records the FAA account as **“there was NO recording of the Phone Bridge”** and separately reports that Ken Mead heard **“there was a recording”** and that it was sent to DOJ. The 2006 OIG report states that the FAA lacked capability to record WOCC telephone lines before September 11 and later recorded most of those lines. Preserve those accounts alongside the December regional interviews and the Herndon Position 14 finding-aid entries. They have different scopes and levels of firsthand knowledge. An endpoint recording or catalog entry cannot establish what was recorded at another endpoint. The original bridge assignment, recorder channels, and any DOJ accession or receipt remain outstanding. [June visit memo, physical p. 2 lines 44–50](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00244.pdf#page=2) · [OIG, physical p. 11 lines 11–18](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=11)
+
+A separately documented FAA recollection says routine FAA hotwash activity **“wasn't done on 9/11.”** The McCartney record describes an assessment **after** the attacks. Their date and scope language is preserved. The account of a post-attack assessment provides a reason to seek the actual report, working notes or equipment inventory, including evidence of its circulation or implementation. [Earlier event-tracker and hotwash review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/36555e33eec89d8cf0370a9f4a7f5e81c8d1c9bc/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-settles-log-and-hotwash-source-2026-11-09/README.md)
+
+## Next records and verification
+
+1. Find the reported Crisis Center assessment, supporting communications and equipment tests, any regional transmittal, and the headquarters DEN requirements and installation or acceptance records. Join only by facility, line or circuit, and date. The New England comparison **ANE-7 / 7601604** and **ANE-8 / 7601605** are separate region records identified in the finding aid. Their inferred media endpoints returned HTTP 404 and their catalog pages did not render through the reader. Their contents remain open. [FAA finding aid, ANE-7 and ANE-8](https://www.archives.gov/research/foia/faa-finding-aid)
+2. Seek the actual Position 14 copy shown at the interview, interviewer notes and playback documentation, and original bridge or recorder assignments. Separately locate the regional action log and received copy of the chronology. Existing source-specific transcript and audio TODOs remain in force.
+3. Keep the corrected May 2003 FAA answer and recipient receipt, the signed action/no-action decision, and each recommendation's response and disposition as the priority case records.
+
+The two five-page PDF bodies were acquired from the NARA media host and match the official index by date, name and extent. Their byte lengths and CRCs agree with the corresponding entries in the held public ZIP member index. These are checks on the documentary copies. The report used selected text passages and five visual crops, with separate checks against the held June memo and OIG report. The reported FAA after-action assessment and the endpoint recordings were outside the executed tests. [Source pins](SOURCE-PINS.json) · [Additive text diff](TEXT-CHANGES.diff)
